{"text":"Multi‑head attention lets the model jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions, preventing the averaging that would occur with a single head.","sources":[1],"quote":"Multi‑head attention allows the model to jointly attend to information from different representation subspaces at different positions. With a single attention head, averaging inhibits this."}
{"text":"The paper uses eight parallel attention heads.","sources":[1],"quote":"In this work we employ h = 8 parallel attention layers, or heads."}
{"text":"Each head projects to dimensions d_k = d_v = d_model/h = 64 when d_model = 512.","sources":[1],"quote":"For each of these we use d_k = d_v = d_model/h = 64."}
{"text":"The overall computational cost remains similar to single‑head attention because each head operates in a reduced dimensionality.","sources":[1],"quote":"Due to the reduced dimension of each head, the total computational cost is similar to that of single‑head attention with full dimensionality."}
{"text":"The multi‑head scheme is applied to encoder‑self‑attention, decoder‑self‑attention and encoder‑decoder attention throughout the 6‑layer stacks.","sources":[3],"quote":"The Transformer uses multi‑head attention in three different ways:"}
